Programming in JavaScript<P>
<HR>
<H1>9  Time and animation</H1>
<HR>
Things on the desktop don't only change when you click or type: clocks tick, and games move by themselves.
In this chapter you'll learn how to make your program do something over and over again, a set number of
times a second, and use it to bounce a ball around a window and to draw a clock that keeps time.<P>

<A NAME=S1></A>
<H2>Timers</H2>
You might think of animating with a loop: move the ball, draw it, wait a little, and go round again. But a
loop that never stops to wait is a problem on the desktop. While your program's code is running, the Wimp
can't do anything else: no other window can be moved or redrawn, and no other program can run. The desktop
would freeze.<P>
A loop can wait politely: <CODE>await sleep(500)</CODE> pauses your program for half a second (500
thousandths of a second) and lets everything else carry on meanwhile, just as <CODE>await input(...)</CODE>
does while you type. That is handy in a program that prints, for a countdown, say. But a desktop program
spends most of its life waiting for something to happen - a click, a key, the next tick - and handling it
quickly, and a loop would get in the way of that. So instead you ask the Wimp to call a function for you,
again and again, with a <I>timer</I>:<P>
<PRE>
task.every(1000, () =&gt; {
  print('tick');
});
</PRE>
<CODE>task.every(ms, fn)</CODE> calls the function <CODE>fn</CODE> every <CODE>ms</CODE> milliseconds (thousandths
of a second), so this prints <I>tick</I> once a second. Between calls, the rest of the desktop gets on with
its work as usual. Each call is quick: it does one step of whatever the program is doing, then returns.<P>
A timer is one of the things that keeps a program running (chapter 6). It stops when the program quits.<P>

<A NAME=S2></A>
<H2>A bouncing ball</H2>
<PRE>
// Bounce - a ball bouncing around a window
const width = 400;
const height = 300;
const size = 15;
let x = 60;
let y = 50;
let dx = 4;    // how far the ball moves across each time
let dy = 3;    // and down
let stop = null;

const w = task.createWindow({
  title: 'Bounce',
  x: 200, y: 150, w: width, h: height,
  extent: { w: width, h: height },
  flags: { title: true, close: true, back: true, moveable: true },
  colours: { workBg: 0 },
  workButton: 'click',
});

w.useCanvas((g) =&gt; {
  g.fillStyle = 'blue';
  g.beginPath();
  g.arc(x, y, size, 0, Math.PI * 2);
  g.fill();
});

function move() {
  x = x + dx;
  y = y + dy;
  if (x &lt; size || x &gt; width - size) {
    dx = -dx;    // hit the left or right: go the other way
  }
  if (y &lt; size || y &gt; height - size) {
    dy = -dy;    // hit the top or bottom
  }
  w.invalidate();
}

// click to stop the ball, and again to start it
w.on('click', () =&gt; {
  if (stop) {
    stop();
    stop = null;
    w.setTitle('Bounce (stopped)');
  } else {
    stop = task.every(20, move);
    w.setTitle('Bounce');
  }
  return true;
});

w.on('close', () =&gt; task.quit());
w.open();
stop = task.every(20, move);
</PRE>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/Bounce" WIDTH=410 HEIGHT=329></CENTER>
The ball flies around the window, bouncing off the edges. Click in the window to stop it, and click again to
start it.<P>

<H3>Moving</H3>
The ball's position is kept in <CODE>x</CODE> and <CODE>y</CODE>, and the distance it moves at each step in
<CODE>dx</CODE> and <CODE>dy</CODE> (<I>d</I> is often used to mean "a change in"). The function
<CODE>move</CODE> is called every 20 milliseconds, which is 50 times a second: often enough for the movement to
look smooth. Each time, it adds <CODE>dx</CODE> to <CODE>x</CODE> and <CODE>dy</CODE> to <CODE>y</CODE>,
then calls <CODE>w.invalidate()</CODE> so the redraw function draws the ball in its new place.<P>

<H3>Bouncing</H3>
When the ball reaches the left or right side of the window, the program makes <CODE>dx</CODE> negative if it
was positive, or positive if it was negative, so the ball goes back the other way:<P>
<PRE>
if (x &lt; size || x &gt; width - size) {
  dx = -dx;
}
</PRE>
The test uses <CODE>size</CODE>, the ball's radius, because <CODE>x</CODE> is the middle of the ball: it
has hit the side when its middle is <CODE>size</CODE> pixels away from it. The top and bottom work the same
way with <CODE>y</CODE> and <CODE>dy</CODE>.<P>
Because <CODE>dx</CODE> is 4 and <CODE>dy</CODE> is 3, the ball moves a little more across than down, and
bounces around the window at an angle. Try other numbers: bigger ones make the ball faster.<P>

<A NAME=S3></A>
<H2>Stopping and starting</H2>
<CODE>task.every</CODE> gives back a function which, when you call it, stops the timer. Bounce keeps it in
the variable <CODE>stop</CODE>:<P>
<PRE>
stop = task.every(20, move);
</PRE>
The click handler checks <CODE>stop</CODE>. If there is a timer, it calls <CODE>stop()</CODE> to end it and
sets <CODE>stop</CODE> to <CODE>null</CODE>, to remember that the ball has stopped. If there isn't one, it starts
a new timer. Using the same variable both to stop the timer and to remember whether one is running keeps the
two from getting out of step.<P>
Notice that <CODE>move</CODE> is given to <CODE>task.every</CODE> by name, without brackets:
<CODE>task.every(20, move)</CODE>. With brackets, <CODE>move()</CODE> would call the function once, straight
away, and give <CODE>task.every</CODE> whatever it returned. Without them, it gives <CODE>task.every</CODE> the
function itself, to call later. You met this idea, passing a function as a value, in chapter 4.<P>

<H3>Doing something once, later</H3>
To do something once, after a delay, use <CODE>task.after(ms, fn)</CODE> instead:<P>
<PRE>
task.after(3000, () =&gt; {
  w.setTitle('Three seconds have passed');
});
</PRE>

<A NAME=S4></A>
<H2>Telling the time</H2>
JavaScript's <CODE>Date</CODE> objects hold a date and time. <CODE>new Date()</CODE> makes one holding the date
and time now, and has functions to get the parts of it:<P>
<PRE>
const now = new Date();
print(now.getHours(), now.getMinutes(), now.getSeconds());
print(now.getDate(), now.getMonth() + 1, now.getFullYear());
print(now.toLocaleTimeString());
</PRE>
<CODE>getHours</CODE> goes from 0 to 23. Watch out for <CODE>getMonth</CODE>: it counts from 0, so January is 0
and December is 11. <CODE>toLocaleTimeString</CODE> gives the time as a string, such as
<CODE>14:05:09</CODE>.<P>

<A NAME=S5></A>
<H2>A clock</H2>
<PRE>
// Clock - an analogue clock
const w = task.createWindow({
  title: 'Clock',
  x: 300, y: 120, w: 260, h: 260,
  extent: { w: 1000, h: 1000 },
  colours: { workBg: 0 },
});

// Draw a hand from the centre: angle in radians clockwise from 12
function hand(g, cx, cy, angle, length, width, colour) {
  g.strokeStyle = colour;
  g.lineWidth = width;
  g.lineCap = 'round';
  g.beginPath();
  g.moveTo(cx, cy);
  g.lineTo(cx + Math.sin(angle) * length,
           cy - Math.cos(angle) * length);
  g.stroke();
}

w.useCanvas((g, r) =&gt; {
  const cx = (r.x0 + r.x1) / 2;
  const cy = (r.y0 + r.y1) / 2;
  const radius = Math.min(r.x1 - r.x0, r.y1 - r.y0) / 2 - 10;

  // the face
  g.fillStyle = wimpColour(12);
  g.strokeStyle = 'black';
  g.lineWidth = 3;
  g.beginPath();
  g.arc(cx, cy, radius, 0, Math.PI * 2);
  g.fill();
  g.stroke();

  // a mark for each hour
  for (let i = 0; i &lt; 12; i++) {
    const a = i * Math.PI / 6;
    g.beginPath();
    g.moveTo(cx + Math.sin(a) * radius * 0.85,
             cy - Math.cos(a) * radius * 0.85);
    g.lineTo(cx + Math.sin(a) * radius,
             cy - Math.cos(a) * radius);
    g.stroke();
  }

  // the hands
  const now = new Date();
  const h = now.getHours() % 12;
  const m = now.getMinutes();
  const s = now.getSeconds();
  const hours = (h + m / 60) * Math.PI / 6;
  const minutes = (m + s / 60) * Math.PI / 30;
  const seconds = s * Math.PI / 30;
  hand(g, cx, cy, hours, radius * 0.5, 6, 'black');
  hand(g, cx, cy, minutes, radius * 0.8, 4, 'black');
  hand(g, cx, cy, seconds, radius * 0.9, 1, 'red');
});

// redraw every second, so the hands move
task.every(1000, () =&gt; {
  w.invalidate();
});

w.on('close', () =&gt; task.quit());
w.open();
</PRE>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/Clock" WIDTH=289 HEIGHT=308></CENTER>
The redraw function draws the face, then the hands in the positions for the time now. Every second, a timer
calls <CODE>w.invalidate()</CODE>, so the face is drawn again and the hands move. As with Pattern in chapter 7,
the clock is drawn to fit the part of the window you can see: resize the window to make the clock bigger.<P>

<H3>Angles round a clock face</H3>
Each hand is drawn by the function <CODE>hand</CODE>, as a line from the centre at an angle measured
clockwise from 12 o'clock. The end of the line is worked out with sine and cosine:<P>
<PRE>
cx + Math.sin(angle) * length,     // across
cy - Math.cos(angle) * length      // up
</PRE>
At 12 o'clock the angle is 0: <CODE>Math.sin(0)</CODE> is 0 and <CODE>Math.cos(0)</CODE> is 1, so the end is
straight above the centre. (It's <CODE>cy - ...</CODE> because <CODE>y</CODE> goes down the window, and the hand
should point up.) At 3 o'clock, a quarter of a turn, the angle is <CODE>Math.PI / 2</CODE>: sine is 1 and
cosine is 0, so the hand points to the right.<P>
The hour marks use the same idea: there are twelve of them, so they are a twelfth of a turn,
<CODE>Math.PI / 6</CODE>, apart.<P>

<H3>The hands</H3>
The minute hand goes round once an hour: a whole turn, <CODE>Math.PI * 2</CODE>, in 60 minutes, which is
<CODE>Math.PI / 30</CODE> for each minute. So that it moves smoothly, the seconds are added in as a fraction of
a minute, <CODE>m + s / 60</CODE>. The hour hand goes round once in twelve hours, a twelfth of a turn,
<CODE>Math.PI / 6</CODE>, each hour, with the minutes added in the same way. (<CODE>% 12</CODE> turns the
24-hour clock's 13 to 23 into 1 to 11.)<P>

<H3>Smoother animation</H3>
For animation that is as smooth as possible, <CODE>task.animate(fn)</CODE> calls <CODE>fn</CODE> every time
the screen itself is redrawn, usually 60 times a second. It gives back a function that stops it, like
<CODE>task.every</CODE>. For most programs, <CODE>task.every</CODE> is all you need.<P>

<A NAME=S6></A>
<H2>Summary</H2>
<UL>
<LI>Never animate with a loop: the desktop would stop while it ran. Use a timer instead.<P>
<LI><CODE>task.every(ms, fn)</CODE> calls <CODE>fn</CODE> every <CODE>ms</CODE> milliseconds, and returns a
function that stops it. <CODE>task.after(ms, fn)</CODE> calls <CODE>fn</CODE> once.<P>
<LI>Animate by changing variables in the timer function and calling <CODE>w.invalidate()</CODE>.<P>
<LI><CODE>new Date()</CODE> is the date and time now; <CODE>getHours</CODE>, <CODE>getMinutes</CODE> and
<CODE>getSeconds</CODE> give its parts.<P>
<LI>For a point at an angle clockwise from straight up: across by <CODE>Math.sin(angle) * length</CODE>, up by
<CODE>Math.cos(angle) * length</CODE>.<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S7></A>
<H2>Exercises</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Add a second ball to Bounce, in a different colour, starting in a different place and moving at a
different speed.<P>
<DT><DD>2  Give Bounce gravity: make the ball speed up as it falls and slow down as it rises, and lose a
little speed each time it hits the floor.<P>
<DT><DD>3  Make Clock show the time in figures as well, below the centre of the face.<P>
<DT><DD>4  Write a countdown program that prints the numbers from 10 down to 1, one a second, then
<I>Lift off!</I>, and then stops its timer.<P>
</DL>

<A NAME=S8></A>
<H2>Answers</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Give the second ball its own variables (<CODE>x2</CODE>, <CODE>y2</CODE>, <CODE>dx2</CODE>,
<CODE>dy2</CODE>), draw it in the redraw function, and move and bounce it in <CODE>move</CODE> exactly as the
first. (With many balls, an array of ball objects is neater: each object holds one ball's
<CODE>x</CODE>, <CODE>y</CODE>, <CODE>dx</CODE> and <CODE>dy</CODE>, and <CODE>move</CODE> goes through them
with <CODE>for...of</CODE>.)<P>
<DT><DD>2  Add to <CODE>dy</CODE> at every step, and handle the floor separately:
<PRE>
  dy = dy + 0.5;                 // gravity
  if (y &gt; height - size) {
    y = height - size;           // don't sink into the floor
    dy = -dy * 0.9;              // bounce, a bit slower
  }
</PRE>
Keep the other tests, but change the one for the top and bottom to test only the top,
<CODE>y &lt; size</CODE>.<P>
<DT><DD>3  At the end of the redraw function:
<PRE>
  g.fillStyle = 'black';
  g.font = '16px sans-serif';
  g.textAlign = 'center';
  g.fillText(now.toLocaleTimeString(), cx, cy + radius / 2);
</PRE>
<CODE>textAlign = 'center'</CODE> makes <CODE>x</CODE> the middle of the text instead of its left end.<P>
<DT><DD>4  Count down in the timer function, and stop the timer at the end:
<PRE>
// Countdown
let count = 10;
const stop = task.every(1000, () =&gt; {
  if (count &gt; 0) {
    print(count);
    count = count - 1;
  } else {
    print('Lift off!');
    stop();
  }
});
</PRE>
The output window stays open when the timer has stopped; close it to end the program.<P>
</DL>

